<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<appendix xml:id="appendixA-ExamReviews" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exam Reviews Page</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
        This page include exam stuff, like exam information that you need to know and review materials for the exams. 
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Exam Dates</title>
        <p>
            Midterm Exam #1 will be in class on <alert>Wednesday, October 21</alert>, from 9:00am to 10:40am. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Midterm Exam #2 will be in class on <alert>Wednesday, November 18</alert>, from 9:00am to 10:40am. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Final Exam will be in class on <alert>Monday, December 7</alert>, from 9:00am to 10:50am. 
        </p>

        <p>
            <em>P.S.:</em> If you have to miss an exam for some reason, reach out to Richard as soon as possible to discuss about the make-up exam. 
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
        The exams will be <alert>closed-book</alert> and <alert>no-notes</alert>.
        A calculator is permitted on the exam. 
        <alert>Remember to show all of your work for full credits. </alert>
        Richard values mathematical reasoning/justification a lot more than just the correct answers. 
        Correct answers with no or minimal work will not receive full credits. Calculator-generated answers without human support will also not receive full credits!
    </p>

    <p>
        Here are some study tips:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Remember we have done a lot of problems in class and in your homework problems. 
                    Make sure you know how to solve the problems we went through in class and in your homework problems. 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Forming a study group is always a good choice to study for math. 
                    Collaborating with your peers and working in a group helps because you can talk through each step together, make sure your solutions are clear to other people, and ask questions when things aren’t perfectly clear.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Asking Richard for help! 
                    Remember he has office hours and you are more than welcome to shoot him questions via email as well!
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Visiting the tutoring center. 
                    The tutoring center uses the <alert>Penji</alert> app located on the sidebar of Canvas to schedule appointments. 
                    The math department also retain graduate students to tutor in the math lounge on the fourth floor of the Fariborz Maseeh Hall.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul> 
    </p>

    <p>
        <alert>Frequently Asked Questions</alert>
    </p>

    <remark>
        <title>What is a good way to study for the exams?</title>

        <p>
            As Richard suggested in the study tips, some of the good ways to study for the exam are to review the homework problems and problems done in class; form a study group with your classmates so you can go over problems with others and bounce of ideas; ask Richard if you have any questions; visit the tutoring center for additional help.  
        </p>

        <p>
            Potentially, there are also a lot of external resources that you can look into for review on the internet. 
        </p>

        <p>
            But remember, Richard is the one writing the exam and grading your work! 
            So you probably want to study the material written by Richard primarily to get to know more about what questions he likes to ask and what answers he expects to see (as opposed to going around the internet blindly without a plan). 
        </p>

        <p>
            Also, make sure you <alert>WORK</alert> on some math problems for review, not just only skimming through stuff. Human learn math through doing, not skimming/reading.
            Just because you can understand how things work doesn't mean you can re-invent the solution on the spot, especially when the exam are timed and weights a lot on your grade. 
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>Do I need a (graphing) calculator on the exam? </title>

        <p>
            Likely yes. 
            It really depends on how comfortable you are solving problems without technology. 
            But there are advantages of having a (graphing) calculator on hand during the exams. 
            It will help you with computations and checking your answers quickly (Yes a graphing calculator can do regular matrix algebra). 
            Also, some students believe that having a calculator can also boost up confidence as well. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Yet, <alert>you must still show all your work for full credits!</alert> 
            Calculators are just a tool to help you do math, not to replace you in the process and your mathematical reasoning/justification. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Calculator-generated answers will not be accepted. 
            So Richard will not accept answers like "the calculator showed that the answer is such and such...". 
            He can't see your calculator screen when he grades your work and this tells him nothing about YOUR understanding of the material. 
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>Does notation matter? Are notations worth any points? </title>

        <p>
            <alert>YES!</alert> 
            Well Richard's research is about students interpretation on notations and their understanding on topics in collegiate level math so you tell me if he cares about notation or not. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Proper notation allows Richard to understand your workflow, increasing your chance for full or partial credit (as opposed to having Richard to guess what you mean on your work... he is bad at guessing). 
            Remember that it is not Richard's responsibility to have to guess what you mean if the work is confusing! 
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>Is there any bonus problem on the exam? </title>

        <p>
            Yes. 
            There will be a bonus problem worth <alert>10% of the exam score</alert> (usually 10 points out of 100). 
        </p>

        <p>
            Coming from a background of math ed that focuses on conceptual understanding and pure math, Richard tends to make the bonus question to focus on the conceptual side... 
            So it is unlikely that you can just apply a formula or a procedure to get the answer. 
            But it won't be something that is completely out of the blue either.
        </p>

        <p>
            Please at least attempt the bonus problem!
            Even if you don't know how to solve it, you can still get some points for showing your thinking and reasoning on the problem (of course, the thinking/reasoning has to be relevant to the problem and make sense).
            There is no penalty for wrong answers on the bonus problem. 
            That's why it is a bonus problem!
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>How many questions can I expect on the exam?</title>
        
        <p>
            Usually, there will be <alert>7 to 8</alert> questions plus a bonus question on the exam, depending on the complexity and the level of difficulty of the questions. 
            The questions will all be (big) free-response questions (like the ones we did on the worksheet). 
            So there won't be any multiple choices or fill-in-the-blank questions. 
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>Will Richard curve exams?</title>

        <p>
            Hmm... not necessarily. 
            While Richard doesn't like to curve the exams, he will do something if the whole class doesn't do well for some reasons beyond their control 
            (some examples include: Richard may accidentally write the exam too hard, a train was going back and forth outside the building that creates waaaaaay too much noise for students to concentrate, a dead squirrel was spotted in the classroom that distracts everyone from taking the exam... well anything is possible!). 
        </p>

        <p>
            If the class average wasn't as high as Richard's expectation, he will do one of the three things and he will poll the class: 
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        administer an optional exam correction, along with a reflection, that allows students to learn from their mistakes and earn some points; 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        simply curve the grade (but how much should he curve... well no one knows it yet); 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        replace one of the midterm exams score with the final score if the final score is higher. 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            But don't rely on these measures to improve your grade! 
            The safest way is to study well and do well on the exams! 
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
        <title>Can I use AI to help me study? </title>

        <p>
            Sure as long as you are using AI to help you learn, NOT replace you in the learning process. 
        </p>

        <p>
            A colleague of mine shared with me her <alert>AI Use Policy for Math</alert> in case you are wondering how AI can (and should) be used in a math class. 
            But she is teaching an online class so some of the items may not make sense in the context of our class... 
            Hope her list helps! 
        </p>

        <p><alert>AI Use Policy for Math</alert></p>
    
        <p>
            AI can be a useful study tool when it strengthens learning rather than replacing thinking. 
            In this course, AI may be used to review concepts, generate additional practice, explain ideas, and help study. 
            It may not be used to complete, check, explain, or rewrite graded course work unless an assignment explicitly states that AI is allowed.
        </p>
        <p>
            AI includes tools such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Microsoft Copilot, NotebookLM, AI-generated search responses, and generative AI features in tools such as Google products and Grammarly.
        </p>

        <p><alert>The Main Rule</alert></p>
        <p>
            AI can help you learn how to do the math. 
            It cannot do, check, explain, or improve the specific math you are submitting for a grade.
        </p>
        <p>
            Do not paste, type, screenshot, photograph, or upload graded course questions into AI.
        </p>

        <p><alert>Acceptable Uses of AI</alert></p>
        <p>AI may be used to:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Explain a general concept in simpler language.</p></li>
            <li><p>Review vocabulary, formulas, or key ideas.</p></li>
            <li><p>Create flashcards, study guides, or practice quizzes.</p></li>
            <li><p>Create new practice problems using different numbers or contexts.</p></li>
            <li><p>Walk through a separate example.</p></li>
            <li><p>Ask guiding questions or provide hints while studying.</p></li>
            <li><p>Help identify concepts that need more practice.</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>Example Prompts</alert></p>
        
        <p><alert>Learn a Concept</alert></p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>"Explain [topic] to me like I am in 5th grade, then give me a simple example."</p></li>
            <li><p>"I am confused about [topic]. Explain how it works, then ask me questions to make sure I understand."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Explain the difference between [concept 1] and [concept 2] using your own examples."</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>Practice and Review</alert></p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>"Quiz me on the vocabulary, formulas, and concepts for [topic]. Ask one question at a time and wait for my answer. If I make a mistake, help me figure out why before giving me another similar question."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Create three new practice problems about [topic], starting easy and getting harder. Let me try each one before showing the solution."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Create a five-question practice quiz over [topic] using completely new questions."</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>Get Help Without Getting the Answer</alert></p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>"I am learning how to solve this type of problem. Give me a new example and guide me through it by asking questions instead of immediately giving me the answer."</p></li>
            <li><p>"I understand the first step but get stuck after that. Teach me the process using your own example."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Give me a hint about what I should think about first, but do not solve the problem for me."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Teach me a strategy for approaching this type of math problem, then give me one to try independently."</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>Study With AI</alert></p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>"Act as my math tutor. Ask me one question at a time about this unit. If I make a mistake, give me a hint before explaining it."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Help me study this topic using retrieval practice. Ask me questions instead of giving me a summary."</p></li>
            <li><p>"Create a study guide from these notes with important concepts, vocabulary, formulas, and new practice questions."</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>Not Allowed Uses of AI</alert></p>
        <p>AI may not be used to:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Upload or enter an assigned Learning Assignment, practice assignment, lab/discussion, quiz, or exam question.</p></li>
            <li><p>Solve or explain an assigned graded question.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check whether an answer to a graded question is correct.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find or fix errors in work being submitted for credit.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write, rewrite, improve, or complete a response that will be submitted for credit.</p></li>
            <li><p>Generate work and then slightly change the wording before submitting it.</p></li>
            <li><p>Be used in any way during a quiz or exam.</p></li>
        </ul>

        <p><alert>What to Do When Stuck</alert></p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Review the guided video notes and worked examples.</p></li>
            <li><p>Return to the lecture video through the Learning Assignment and use Practice mode.</p></li>
            <li><p>Try a similar practice problem.</p></li>
            <li><p>Post in the Q&amp;A board.</p></li>
            <li><p>Email me with a screenshot and explain where the difficulty began.</p></li>
            <li><p>Attend office/student hours or work with PCC tutoring.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use AI to learn the general concept or practice with a different problem, not to work on the assigned graded question.</p></li>
        </ul>
        
        <p>
            The goal is not to prevent the use of AI. The goal is to use it in ways that strengthen learning while making sure graded work reflects each student's own understanding and skills.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
        Below are some review materials for the exams. 
        Feel free to check them out and use them for your review!
    </p>
  </introduction>

